---
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'

const installedExample = `import { launchBrowser } from '@suitecut/browser-control'

const browser = await launchBrowser({
  backend: 'cdp',
  executablePath: '/path/to/google-chrome',
  headless: true,
  viewport: { width: 1440, height: 900 },
})

try {
  const context = await browser.newContext()
  const page = await context.newPage()
  await page.goto('https://example.com')
  console.log(await page.getByRole('heading').innerText())
  await page.setViewportSize({ width: 1280, height: 720 })
  await page.screenshot({ path: 'page.png' })
} finally {
  await browser.close()
}`

const firefoxExample = `import { launchProtocolBrowser } from '@suitecut/browser-control/remote'

const firefox = await launchProtocolBrowser({
  backend: 'bidi',
  executablePath: '/path/to/firefox',
  headless: true,
  viewport: { width: 1280, height: 720 },
})
try {
  const page = await firefox.newPage()
  await page.goto('https://example.com')
  console.log(await page.title())
} finally {
  await firefox.close()
}`

const attachExample = `import { connectBrowser } from '@suitecut/browser-control/remote'

const chrome = await connectBrowser({
  backend: 'cdp',
  endpointURL: 'http://127.0.0.1:9222',
  timeoutMs: 15_000,
})
console.log((await chrome.pages()).length)
await chrome.close()

const firefox = await connectBrowser({
  backend: 'bidi',
  endpointURL: 'ws://127.0.0.1:9223/session',
})
await firefox.close()`

const cdpExtras = `await page.frameLocator('iframe[title="Billing"]').getByLabel('Account number').fill('12345')
await page.getByLabel('Supporting document').setInputFiles('/absolute/path/document.pdf')

const pending = page.waitForEvent('download')
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Export' }).click()
const download = await pending
await download.saveAs('/absolute/path/export.csv')

page.once('dialog', async (dialog) => {
  await dialog.accept()
})`

const viewerCli = `npx browser-control view https://example.com \\
  --executable /absolute/path/to/suitecut-browser \\
  --resolution auto`

const viewerExample = `import { launchBrowser, createBrowserViewer } from '@suitecut/browser-control'

const browser = await launchBrowser({
  executablePath: '/absolute/path/to/suitecut-browser',
  headless: true,
  pixelFormat: 'bgra',
})
const page = await browser.newPage()
await page.goto('https://example.com')
const viewer = await createBrowserViewer({ browser, page, resolutionMode: 'auto' })
console.log(viewer.url)

try {
  // One fully awaited agent step at a time. Each step waits during human control.
  await viewer.runAgent(() => page.getByRole('link', { name: 'More information' }).click())
} finally {
  await viewer.close()
  await browser.close()
}`

const buildCommands = `pnpm --filter @suitecut/browser-control build
pnpm --filter @suitecut/browser-control pack --pack-destination /absolute/output/directory
npm install /absolute/path/to/suitecut-browser-control-2.0.0.tgz`
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Browser control"
  description="Drive the suitecut CEF engine, installed Chromium browsers or Firefox from Node.js, with a live viewer and human handover."
  section="browser-control"
>
  <h1>Browser control</h1>
  <p>
    <code>@suitecut/browser-control</code> is the standalone Node.js SDK for browsers. It doesn't depend
    on suitecut's narration, rendering or test runner, and its protocol adapters have no external automation
    dependencies. It needs Node.js 22 or newer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="backends">Backends</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>cef</code>, the default: suitecut's embedded engine, with native capture and the live
      viewer.
    </li>
    <li><code>cdp</code>: an installed Chrome, Chromium, Edge or Brave over CDP.</li>
    <li><code>bidi</code>: an installed Firefox over WebDriver BiDi.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">
    The package doesn't bundle or download browsers. Safari and WebKit aren't supported.
  </p>

  <h2 id="installed-browsers">Installed browsers</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code>backend</code> and point <code>executablePath</code> at the browser. Owned launches use
    a temporary profile and a loopback debugging endpoint; both are cleaned up on close. Sandboxing stays
    on unless you pass an argument that turns it off.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={installedExample} />
  <p>
    For protocol-only apps, <code>@suitecut/browser-control/remote</code> exports
    <code>launchProtocolBrowser</code> and <code>connectBrowser</code> without loading the CEF module
    graph.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={firefoxExample} />

  <h2 id="attach">Attach to a running browser</h2>
  <p>
    <code>connectBrowser</code> attaches to a browser whose debugging endpoint is already enabled. CDP
    accepts an HTTP origin or a browser WebSocket URL; Firefox needs its WebSocket session endpoint. <code
      >close()</code
    > disconnects without shutting the browser down unless you set
    <code>closeBrowserOnClose: true</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={attachExample} />

  <h2 id="protocol-features">Protocol features</h2>
  <p>
    Both protocol backends support tabs, isolated contexts where available, cookies, navigation,
    evaluation, screenshots, viewport changes, CSS, role, text, label and test-id locators, form
    controls, and browser-dispatched keyboard and pointer input. Check
    <code>browser.capabilities()</code> for what a backend supports, and use
    <code>browser.sendCommand()</code> for raw protocol commands.
  </p>
  <p>
    CDP also supports iframes, file uploads, downloads, JavaScript dialogs, popups, hover, dragging
    and scrolling. Subscribe to downloads before triggering them; dialogs without a listener are
    dismissed.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={cdpExtras} />

  <h2 id="viewer">Live viewer</h2>
  <p>
    With the CEF backend, the viewer streams the same browser session to a local web page. It binds
    to <code>127.0.0.1</code> behind a private link; treat that link as a credential and forward the port
    over SSH for remote machines.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code={viewerCli} />
  <p>
    <code>--resolution auto</code> fits the page to the viewer while a person has control. Fixed sizes
    such as <code>1920x1080</code> are also available, up to 3840 × 2160 physical pixels. Resizing changes
    the page's real CSS viewport, not just the displayed image.
  </p>

  <h2 id="handover">Human handover</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Take control</strong> waits for admitted agent work to finish, then hands input to a person.
    <strong>Resume AI</strong> releases held keys and buttons and returns control. Both use the same page,
    cookies and storage.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={viewerExample} />
  <p>
    During human control, direct SDK calls fail explicitly. <code>viewer.runAgent()</code> waits for control
    instead, so submit bounded steps through it. Up to eight clients can watch, with one owning input.
  </p>

  <h2 id="build">Build and install</h2>
  <p>
    The package is built and packed from the repository; it hasn't been published to npm. Install
    the tarball in your app and distribute the CEF runtime separately.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code={buildCommands} />
</DocsLayout>
